# sweep-2d.steps -- render-regression sweep for the 2D path: every HUD element,
# menu and console line, all drawn through gl_shader_2d (engine/h2shared/gl_draw.c).
#
# Needs no map and no game data beyond the demo, so it is the cheap one (~25s):
#
#   nix build .#demodata -o result-demodata
#   nix shell nixpkgs#xorg-server nixpkgs#xdotool nixpkgs#imagemagick \
#             nixpkgs#bubblewrap --command \
#     env STEPS=tools/sweep-2d.steps \
#     ./tools/headless-drive.sh script /tmp/2d-new result-demodata/share/hexenwail
#
# Compare against a reference run of the unmodified build, MASKING BOTH cursors:
#
#   nix shell nixpkgs#imagemagick --command \
#     ./tools/shot-diff.sh /tmp/2d-ref /tmp/2d-new 34x62+288+56 12x12+20+572
#
# Unlike the world-culling sweep, this path IS visible, so pixels are the right
# gate here rather than a numeric proxy.
#
# THE MASK IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND THE REASON IS A TRAP WORTH KNOWING.  The menu
# cursor is an animated sprite whose position also tracks the selected row, so
# a frame is only reproducible when two runs sample the same animation phase.
# Measured on an unmodified binary: runs A and B agreed on all four frames with
# 0 differing pixels -- and run C disagreed with A by 26, 16 and 23 pixels.
# Every differing pixel lay inside 25x20+291+60 (frame 01) or 15x19+295+80
# (frame 02, cursor one row down).  34x62+288+56 covers both.
#
# THERE ARE TWO BLINKING CURSORS HERE, NOT ONE, and the second one was found the
# expensive way -- as a 15.78-pixel "regression" in a change that had none.  The
# two Escapes before frame 04 close the menu outright, so 04 is the CONSOLE, and
# console.c:595 draws the input-line cursor only while (int)(realtime*4)&1.  It
# is a two-state blink, not a slow animation, so it hides from a small sample:
# four runs of one binary produced BOTH states, and one of them was byte-exact
# against a reference taken before the change.  Region 12x12+20+572, measured as
# a 9x9+21+574 bounding box.
#
# So a gate calibrated on ONE pair of runs can look perfect and be worthless.
# If you add frames here, re-derive the mask from at least three runs of the
# SAME binary before trusting a zero -- and note that agreeing runs prove
# nothing either, since a two-state blink can land the same way twice.

sleep 6
shot 01-main-menu
key Down
sleep 1
shot 02-menu-moved
key Return
sleep 2
shot 03-submenu
key Escape
sleep 1
key Escape
sleep 1
shot 04-back-to-main
